WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:03.879
In this lesson, we're going to take a look at lighting tools

2
00:00:03.879 --> 00:00:06.960
and we're going to interactively light a hero object.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:10.320
Feel free to follow along for this part of the lesson.

4
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.300
So let's get started.

5
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.779
So for this lesson, I went and opened up here a VST.

6
00:00:15.779 --> 00:00:18.420
There is a hero end, which is a simple example

7
00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:19.980
of what you can do to get your lighting

8
00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:21.320
in the right direction.

9
00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:23.280
But we're going to go ahead and build our own.

10
00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:24.699
Now you'll see a few things in here

11
00:00:24.699 --> 00:00:25.980
if you have some lights floating around,

12
00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.260
go and just kill them.

13
00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.539
I might have my sky in here still,

14
00:00:30.609 --> 00:00:32.570
just a few little things in here.

15
00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:37.600
So feel free to remove them completely.

16
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.259
And what we also want to do,

17
00:00:40.259 --> 00:00:41.619
and you'll notice the difference in mind

18
00:00:41.619 --> 00:00:43.179
is I have a glowing material.

19
00:00:43.179 --> 00:00:45.460
So I'm just going to briefly talk about that for a second.

20
00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:50.100
I simply just grabbed the material for the windshield.

21
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.740
So if I select the object itself,

22
00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:57.070
you will see the windshield material in here

23
00:00:57.070 --> 00:00:59.310
under details, let's go back up.

24
00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:00.829
There we go, went too far.

25
00:01:00.829 --> 00:01:03.149
And you'll see it's element three.

26
00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:04.909
That element three, I simply just went in here

27
00:01:04.909 --> 00:01:07.750
and connected that color to the emissive right there.

28
00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:09.989
And then I just hit save or apply.

29
00:01:09.989 --> 00:01:11.189
Apply it to see if you like it,

30
00:01:11.189 --> 00:01:13.579
save if you want to keep it.

31
00:01:13.579 --> 00:01:16.099
So let's go ahead and get started here

32
00:01:16.099 --> 00:01:17.859
and actually remove a few more things.

33
00:01:17.859 --> 00:01:20.290
I'm going to get rid of my volumetric cloud.

34
00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:22.280
I'm going to hit delete on that.

35
00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:24.040
I'm going to get rid of my skylight.

36
00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:27.719
I'm going to get rid of my directional light

37
00:01:27.719 --> 00:01:30.450
as well as my exponential light fog.

38
00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:33.189
So we're going to use that tool we used earlier on.

39
00:01:33.189 --> 00:01:34.150
I'm going to go to windows

40
00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:38.060
and I'm going to go to my environment light mixer.

41
00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:42.810
In here, I can quickly get up and running

42
00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:45.489
by clicking on my create skylight,

43
00:01:45.489 --> 00:01:47.590
create atmospheric light,

44
00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:50.370
create a volumetric cloud and create a high fog.

45
00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:51.969
You might get an error at first

46
00:01:51.969 --> 00:01:53.450
because there are some dependencies

47
00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:54.810
and it will tell you on real say,

48
00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:57.409
hey man, I need the environment in here

49
00:01:57.409 --> 00:01:58.790
and you don't have it yet.

50
00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:00.290
Just go ahead and keep clicking

51
00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:02.290
and you should get those in the right direction.

52
00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:04.489
Now, if you ever needed another directional light,

53
00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:06.799
let's say I created another directional light

54
00:02:06.799 --> 00:02:07.799
and I drag it on the scene.

55
00:02:07.799 --> 00:02:09.960
Unreal is going to say like, man, what are you doing?

56
00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:10.939
I don't understand.

57
00:02:10.939 --> 00:02:13.400
Which one do you want to affect the fog?

58
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:14.780
In that case, you want to go ahead

59
00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:15.960
and in that directional light,

60
00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.060
just type in forward shading

61
00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:20.699
and then change the priority order.

62
00:02:20.699 --> 00:02:22.419
And you can do that with either directional light

63
00:02:22.419 --> 00:02:24.830
and that will solve that issue.

64
00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:26.710
So let's go ahead in our directional light

65
00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:29.300
that we have in here already.

66
00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:33.740
Let me move some of these around

67
00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:37.360
just to sort between them.

68
00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:38.360
There you go.

69
00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:39.719
We're going to lower this directional light

70
00:02:40.599 --> 00:02:42.520
to a very low angle.

71
00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:44.919
Now, the nice thing about this reacting to our environment,

72
00:02:44.919 --> 00:02:46.039
if I wanted more red light,

73
00:02:46.039 --> 00:02:48.699
I can make it look like a sunset

74
00:02:48.699 --> 00:02:51.060
or if I want to just pitch black dark, I can,

75
00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:53.500
or maybe I just want to pull in some of that cool light.

76
00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:56.340
So to do that, I can just make it a little bit higher

77
00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:59.439
and in here, just slightly orange with the light.

78
00:02:59.439 --> 00:03:02.020
In here, I can just lower my amount

79
00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:04.930
to maybe make it 0.5.

80
00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:06.129
There you go.

81
00:03:06.129 --> 00:03:09.150
And even lower, we can do 0.2.

82
00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:12.030
So most of our light is dependent upon what we throw in

83
00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:14.870
after the fact, such as creating a key light,

84
00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:16.270
fill light and back lights

85
00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:19.169
and any rim lighting that we want to add.

86
00:03:19.169 --> 00:03:20.949
So we have that up and running.

87
00:03:20.949 --> 00:03:23.330
We also want to make sure we're using Lumen.

88
00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:25.449
So always check your scene when you can

89
00:03:25.449 --> 00:03:28.210
and that requires going into your project settings.

90
00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:32.000
And like we mentioned before, going under rendering

91
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.889
and you want to make sure that Lumen is on.

92
00:03:34.889 --> 00:03:39.919
And we have Lumen here set for global dynamic method.

93
00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:41.990
We also have reflections Lumen.

94
00:03:41.990 --> 00:03:44.650
We're also looking at our ray lighting mode.

95
00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:46.150
I'm setting it kind of high

96
00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:48.830
for our hit lighting for reflections.

97
00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:51.710
And then I'm also setting my software ray tracing mode

98
00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:53.810
to detail tracing.

99
00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:55.169
To do that though, you want to make sure

100
00:03:55.169 --> 00:03:58.360
you're using hardware ray tracing when available.

101
00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:00.120
And also with that, you want to make sure

102
00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:03.680
that you also have supports hardware ray tracing overall.

103
00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:06.599
This is going to be very helpful to work with your scene

104
00:04:06.599 --> 00:04:10.159
because Unreal's Lumen does borrow some of that tech.

105
00:04:10.159 --> 00:04:15.159
Now you can use it without these fallbacks

106
00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:19.259
but you're not going to get as efficient workflows

107
00:04:19.259 --> 00:04:21.000
and you're not going to get the precise stuff

108
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.600
that you would get borrowing

109
00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:23.839
some of that ray tracing technology.

110
00:04:23.839 --> 00:04:25.720
And you really want to try to work

111
00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:27.560
with a graphics card that has it.

112
00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:29.759
Again, virtual shadow maps are on by default

113
00:04:29.759 --> 00:04:31.040
and also make sure in your scenes

114
00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:33.040
especially if you're getting it from the marketplace

115
00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:35.160
make sure that you have SM6 active.

116
00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:36.959
This is going to support your virtual shadows

117
00:04:36.959 --> 00:04:39.160
but mainly also going to support your visualizers

118
00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:40.839
for them to even come up, such as looking

119
00:04:40.839 --> 00:04:45.600
through your Nanite and your virtual shadow map visualizer.

120
00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:46.519
So I'm going to close this out

121
00:04:46.519 --> 00:04:48.439
just some things to remember.

122
00:04:48.439 --> 00:04:50.480
And now we need a post-process volume.

123
00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:51.439
So I'm going to go down

124
00:04:51.439 --> 00:04:53.800
and go to my quickly add to project.

125
00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:54.779
I'm going to go to my volumes.

126
00:04:54.779 --> 00:04:57.180
I'm going to go to that post-process volume.

127
00:04:57.180 --> 00:04:59.500
You can also type it in into get context.

128
00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:02.160
So I can type in post-process volume.

129
00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:04.399
You'll see it right there.

130
00:05:04.399 --> 00:05:05.680
You also want to make sure

131
00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:08.540
let's do the tick boxes here real quick.

132
00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:11.259
We want to make sure they're infinite extended bound.

133
00:05:11.259 --> 00:05:12.660
It's got a little bit kind of almost

134
00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:16.360
towards the middle and turn that on.

135
00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:18.720
We want to make sure that we're running completely

136
00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:19.759
with Lumen.

137
00:05:19.759 --> 00:05:21.680
So turn on your reflections for Lumen

138
00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:24.180
and turn on your global elimination for Lumen.

139
00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:28.100
From there, we can turn on these for quality control.

140
00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:29.540
Now I'm not going to crank these too high.

141
00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:30.500
We'll leave them by default.

142
00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:32.779
I think you get the idea of how to adjust these.

143
00:05:32.779 --> 00:05:34.740
So don't, we need to spend a lot of time fussing

144
00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:35.579
with our settings.

145
00:05:35.579 --> 00:05:37.100
That's something that you can do to get your image

146
00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:40.069
and your scene exactly the way that you want.

147
00:05:40.829 --> 00:05:43.069
I'm also going to turn on my Lumen reflections

148
00:05:43.069 --> 00:05:46.689
and I'm going to turn on the hit aligning for reflections.

149
00:05:46.689 --> 00:05:48.230
Get us our highest quality.

150
00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:50.029
Don't have to worry too much about translucency.

151
00:05:50.029 --> 00:05:52.410
There's really isn't anything translucent here.

152
00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:54.310
And then we'll have a max reflection bounces.

153
00:05:54.310 --> 00:05:57.110
So if you want to, we can increase that to maybe two

154
00:05:57.110 --> 00:05:58.649
or three, if you want.

155
00:05:58.649 --> 00:06:00.540
We'll do three in this case.

156
00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:02.240
So you'll see a bit of noise going on

157
00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:03.279
in our scene initially.

158
00:06:03.279 --> 00:06:05.180
And that's because some of these glowing objects

159
00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:08.879
such as these small ones over here, like in this angle

160
00:06:08.879 --> 00:06:09.779
you'll see a lot of noise.

161
00:06:10.339 --> 00:06:11.180
You don't see as much noise here,

162
00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:12.540
but you see a lot of it here

163
00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:14.180
because that's a small glowing object

164
00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:16.660
and a real kind of has a little bit of problem with that.

165
00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:18.220
Now, if we wanted to, we can go and replace

166
00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:20.379
that material to prevent that

167
00:06:20.379 --> 00:06:23.620
and replace that material for that object

168
00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:26.259
or even change the material of the vehicle

169
00:06:26.259 --> 00:06:29.060
and edit it in some way where there's less roughness.

170
00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:30.180
We can all do that.

171
00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:33.120
And we talked about that before previously.

172
00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:34.800
We can also in our post-process volume

173
00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:36.279
just a little quick nugget.

174
00:06:36.279 --> 00:06:38.680
We can also control our exposure.

175
00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:41.379
Now this is covered in other classes,

176
00:06:41.379 --> 00:06:42.540
but I'll briefly talk about it.

177
00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:44.680
I'm going to leave on auto exposure.

178
00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:45.639
With that auto exposure,

179
00:06:45.639 --> 00:06:47.990
I'll turn on my minimum and my maximum.

180
00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:50.110
You'll see the auto exposure adjust itself.

181
00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:53.759
And we're going to lock that down to a one and a one.

182
00:06:53.759 --> 00:06:55.259
And that's a good place to work from there.

183
00:06:55.259 --> 00:06:57.079
And that's just a quick rundown with that.

184
00:06:57.079 --> 00:06:58.240
If you want to adjust it more,

185
00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:00.199
there's a exposure compensation

186
00:07:00.199 --> 00:07:03.819
and I can adjust a slider to what I need.

187
00:07:03.819 --> 00:07:04.899
I can leave it by default.

188
00:07:04.899 --> 00:07:08.360
That way we're pushing more stuff through the light.

189
00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:11.240
So now let's go ahead and pull in a Rec Light.

190
00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:13.120
There's a Rec Light.

191
00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:14.920
One thing I want to mention with the Rec Light,

192
00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:16.899
you want to make sure that you're not pushing

193
00:07:16.899 --> 00:07:18.800
your Rec Lights through geometry.

194
00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:20.779
You will get noise when it comes to ray tracing.

195
00:07:20.779 --> 00:07:23.240
And there is a little bit of noise that may peak in

196
00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:28.000
when using a Rec Light in a Lumen scenario,

197
00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:30.339
because it's using some of that information.

198
00:07:30.339 --> 00:07:31.800
It's using those photons,

199
00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:33.860
calculating and then bouncing it back to the camera.

200
00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:35.920
So try not to shove them into geometry.

201
00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:37.779
It also doesn't make too much sense to do so,

202
00:07:37.779 --> 00:07:40.259
because you will get a hard line anyway.

203
00:07:40.259 --> 00:07:42.500
Want to make sure this light is movable.

204
00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:44.300
And then from there, we don't want to control.

205
00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:49.920
We want to control our source width and our source height.

206
00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:52.319
Let's go and move this up.

207
00:07:52.319 --> 00:07:55.699
And we can also angle this if we need to.

208
00:07:55.699 --> 00:07:58.879
And I can move it back just a little bit.

209
00:07:58.879 --> 00:07:59.720
There we go.

210
00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:01.579
A little mood lighting for that.

211
00:08:01.579 --> 00:08:05.279
Pretty nice.

212
00:08:05.279 --> 00:08:09.100
Let's go ahead and pull in a Fill Light here.

213
00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:11.699
Now the Fill Light is going to be another Rec Light.

214
00:08:11.699 --> 00:08:13.889
So we'll pull that in,

215
00:08:13.889 --> 00:08:18.449
pull it off to the side, a little moodiness going on.

216
00:08:18.449 --> 00:08:22.699
We'll do a 90 degree angle again.

217
00:08:22.699 --> 00:08:24.040
Move that over.

218
00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:25.720
Move that up.

219
00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:26.899
Ooh, nice.

220
00:08:26.899 --> 00:08:28.449
Little subtle.

221
00:08:28.449 --> 00:08:30.230
Move that over here just a little bit more.

222
00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:32.250
Turn off the cast shadows.

223
00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:33.350
Set that to movable.

224
00:08:33.350 --> 00:08:34.409
Very important,

225
00:08:34.409 --> 00:08:38.679
because it's going to update for us correctly with the GI.

226
00:08:38.679 --> 00:08:40.799
We're going to control the width

227
00:08:40.799 --> 00:08:42.639
and we're going to control the height.

228
00:08:42.639 --> 00:08:44.159
Now you'll notice the shape is showing up.

229
00:08:44.159 --> 00:08:45.000
See that?

230
00:08:46.139 --> 00:08:47.259
Kind of don't want that.

231
00:08:47.259 --> 00:08:50.980
So to be able to dumb that down, you can use Spec.

232
00:08:50.980 --> 00:08:52.759
We can go into the Spec and lower that.

233
00:08:52.759 --> 00:08:55.379
Now you're going to lose some of those little shiny bits,

234
00:08:55.379 --> 00:08:57.659
but we don't really have a lot using this light.

235
00:08:57.659 --> 00:08:58.500
So we're okay.

236
00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:00.340
We're not using this for rim lighting.

237
00:09:00.340 --> 00:09:02.919
So we don't have to worry about that too much.

238
00:09:02.919 --> 00:09:04.440
I'm going to move this over.

239
00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:06.600
I'm going to rotate it

240
00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:08.240
and I'm going to give it a different color.

241
00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:09.679
Let's have some fun.

242
00:09:09.679 --> 00:09:10.500
So I'm going to go in here

243
00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:12.899
and make this kind of like almost a purple.

244
00:09:12.899 --> 00:09:14.700
Little cyberpunk looking.

245
00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:19.039
Purplish pink, I guess you could say.

246
00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:20.480
And you can also pluck that color.

247
00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:23.799
And what I mean by that is clicking on the color wheel,

248
00:09:23.799 --> 00:09:25.240
you can actually use a dropper

249
00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:29.370
and you can choose what color you want.

250
00:09:29.370 --> 00:09:32.360
So I'm going to make it a little bit stronger.

251
00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:33.919
Have some fun with that.

252
00:09:33.919 --> 00:09:35.960
And then now let's go ahead and make some rim lighting.

253
00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:37.519
Now the rim lighting, if we're using a Rec light,

254
00:09:37.519 --> 00:09:38.480
we got to be careful

255
00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:42.360
because it may not quite be exactly

256
00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:43.200
the way they want to tackle it.

257
00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:44.480
And what I mean by that

258
00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:47.220
is that shape could still give us some trouble.

259
00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:49.059
What we could do is dumb down the reflectivity

260
00:09:49.059 --> 00:09:50.960
on anything nearby to prevent it.

261
00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:53.559
Or again, like we did, control the spec.

262
00:09:53.559 --> 00:09:56.840
So let's go ahead and pull that Rec light in.

263
